tact Information (form MC-040) ↗️ (link opens in a new tab).\n\nOver the coming months, the court may send you information about court dates. The plaintiff will often send you requests for information (discovery), like:\n\n  * Requests for admission\n  * Form interrogatories\n\nIf you get these, you need to respond to the request.  If you do not respond, you could lose your case or end up owing more money. You may also get a Declaration in Lieu of Live Testimony. \n\nOnce you've filed your forms, you've completed the first step in responding to a debt lawsuit.\n\nDebt lawsuits\n\n## What's next?\n\nNow that you've filed a response, you'll start to prepare your defense by using a legal process called discovery to gather evidence and find out what evidence the other side has. \n\n  * Go back to an overview Return to an overview of the entire process for debt lawsuits and navigate to any part.\n\n  * Prepare for your trial Get instructions for preparing for your trial, including gathering evidence and finding out what evidence the other side has.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGuide to court forms | California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/court-basics/find-fill-out-forms)\nciteturn1686search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; The California Rules of Court, starting with rule 2.100 ↗️, tell you what is required for any documents you file with the court. ... You can use Notice of Change of Address or other Contact Information (form MC-040) ↗️ .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/find-forms?page=12&query=TH-)\nciteturn1686search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   * MC-040 Notice of Change of Address or Other Contact Effective: January 1, 2013 ...   * MC-050 * Substitution of Attorney—Civil (Without Court Order) Effective: January 1, 2009\n---------------------------------------